review.detail:
The two sibling reports that read contains/doesNotContain against a category-derived axis were not fixed, so both still diverge from the query in the money-losing direction that is the whole point of this ticket.

`packages/desktop-client/src/components/reports/spreadsheets/sankey-spreadsheet.ts:459`

Evidence: filterCategoryGroups (lines 429-503, called at line 328 on the Sankey report's category-derived node set) still reads text operators literally:
    if (op === 'contains') {
      return typeof value === 'string' && name.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase());
    }
    if (op === 'doesNotContain') {
      return typeof value === 'string' && !name.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase());
    }
while transaction-rules.ts:641-649 compiles the same condition to $like '%'+value+'%' -> UNICODE_LIKE. Executed against a three-category fixture (Food, Rent, Utilities):
    sankey, Category contains "%"  -> []
    sankey, Category contains "?" -> []
    sankey, Category contains "o%" -> []
The query returns every category for each of those values (% -> .*.* matches everything; o% -> .*o.* matches Food), so a fetched category has no node to render into and its money leaves every total — the identical defect class as BUG-2, on a report the ticket did not name. The work order's own consumer enumeration admits the gap: "I did not grep for LIKE-pattern evaluation elsewhere."

Fix: Route both branches through likePatternToRegex(getNormalisedString('%' + value + '%')).test(getNormalisedString(name)), matching what budgetDataQuery.ts:246-265 now does, and add the same differential cases to sankey-spreadsheet's tests.

budget-analysis-spreadsheet.ts carries the same unfixed literal-substring reading of contains/doesNotContain, and unlike Sankey it feeds a per-month category list that the report then sums.

`packages/desktop-client/src/components/reports/spreadsheets/budget-analysis-spreadsheet.ts:246`

Evidence: Lines 246-256 are byte-for-byte the pre-fix budgetDataQuery.ts shape:
    } else if (cond.op === 'contains') {
      return typeof cond.value === 'string' && textValue.toLowerCase().includes(cond.value.toLowerCase());
    } else if (cond.op === 'doesNotContain') {
      return typeof cond.value === 'string' && !textValue.toLowerCase().includes(cond.value.toLowerCase());
    }
The result feeds categoriesToInclude (lines 210, 267, 285, 316) into summarizeMonthCategories(monthData, categoriesToInclude) at line 339 — it decides which categories get a cell in the analysis grid and therefore what the report's totals are made of. Same direction as the other two: the axis is a strict subset of the query, so the money silently leaves.

Fix: Same change as the Sankey site: delegate to likePatternToRegex with the normalised '%'-wrapped value, and cover it with the differential cases.

