{
  "issue": 4,
  "version": 3,
  "understanding": "QA's own retest says BUG-1 is fixed: T-4, T-5 and T-6 all pass, the 'any of' axis no longer collapses, and the money is no longer dropped. canNarrowAxisByConditions does exactly what it was asked to do. What the previous attempt got wrong is the completeness of its own premise, and that is what produced both new bugs (BUG-2, BUG-3).\n\nIt reasoned about the axis/query divergence along ONE dimension only: which field a condition addresses. Its gate asks 'is every disjunct addressed to the category axis?' and, when the answer is yes, it lets the axis narrow. That question is necessary but not sufficient. It silently assumes that once a disjunct is about the category axis, the axis's reading of it and the query's reading of it are the same set. They are not, because they are two different implementations of the same operator. filterCategoriesByConditions implements `contains` as a JavaScript substring test - textValue.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase()) - while conditionsToAQL (packages/loot-core/src/server/transactions/transaction-rules.ts:641-649) compiles the same condition to '$like' with the pattern '%' + value + '%', which the AQL compiler emits as UNICODE_LIKE(getNormalisedString(right), NORMALISE(left)) (compiler.ts:733-736) and unicodeLike evaluates by translating the pattern into a JavaScript regex in which '%' is '.*' and '?' is '.' (unicodeLike.ts:7-31). One side is a literal string search, the other is a pattern language. A value of '%' matches no category name literally and matches every category name as a pattern.\n\nSo BUG-2 needs no 'any of' at all: a single `Category contains %` under 'all of' narrows the axis to zero rows while the query returns the entire budget, and every cent is discarded with no error and no empty state. The gate never sees it because it only inspects field, and 'category' is a category field. BUG-3 is the same divergence seen under 'or', where both disjuncts are category fields so the gate says yes and the axis narrows to Bills while the '%' disjunct makes the query match everything - BUG-1's exact failure mode, arriving through an operator the gate does not screen. The gate was never going to catch either one, and adding operator conditions to it would treat the symptom: it would decline to narrow, leaving a legitimate filter showing empty rows forever, without the axis and the query ever agreeing.\n\nThe fix is therefore not another gate. It is to make the axis evaluate category text operators through the same primitives the query uses, so the two agree by construction rather than by argument.\n\nI reproduced the divergence by execution rather than by reading QA's error string. I hand-ported likePatternToRegex, getNormalisedString and the sqlite `regexp` into a scratch node script and ran the axis's current evaluation (taken verbatim from the module QA captured as served at commit 1fa6be94, qa-evidence/qa-evidence/served-budgetDataQuery.ts:99-140) against the compiled query's evaluation over 21 category names x the operator/value set. `contains '%'` keeps 1 of 21 on the axis against 21 on the query, `contains '?'` keeps 0, `contains '\\%'` keeps 0 of the 1 it should, and `contains 'e'` misses 'Café'; on the `matches` side the axis is wider than the query for 'RE', 're', 'C' and 'café', and narrower for the 601-character value. Every divergence is a strict subset in at least one direction, and the subset direction is the one that silently discards money. '%' dropping all but one is exactly QA's 0.00 on Split=Category while Split=Payee - an axis built from payees, so unnarrowed - kept the baseline.\n\nTwo things in the QA report are wrong, and I checked both rather than inheriting them. First, BUG-2's suspected_cause says 'The same holds for _' and the coverage-gap list says '_ behaves exactly like % and will reproduce BUG-2'. It does not. This codebase's UNICODE_LIKE is not SQLite's LIKE: it is a JS function that builds a regex and treats only '%' and '?' as wildcards, so '_' is an ordinary literal - unicodeLike('X_', 'CSharp') is 0 and the axis and query agree on '_' and on 'C_' alike. A fix that escapes '_' would be escaping a character the query treats literally, and would introduce a divergence where none exists. Second, the report reasons that `matches` can only make the axis wider than the query, which holds for a case-mismatched value (the query's $regexp is compiled as REGEXP(right, left) with no NORMALISE and no /i, compiler.ts:737-740, so a case-sensitive match against the raw name, while the axis uses /i) but is false for a value longer than 256 characters: the axis's `condition.value.length <= 256` guard rejects it for every category, and the query's REGEXP has no such limit. That guard is the same money-losing shape as BUG-2, reachable from the report's own filter UI - there is no maxLength anywhere in FiltersMenu.tsx or GenericInput.tsx. I am fixing it in the same stroke and AC-16 exists so it cannot come back.\n\nEverything QA validated is kept: canNarrowAxisByConditions stays exactly as it is, because once the axis and the query agree on operator semantics there is nothing left for it to screen, and BUG-3's report that 'the gate returns true' is not the defect - the divergence underneath it was. AC-1 through AC-13 are carried forward unchanged and four more are appended.\n\nThis revision answers one objection, and it is about this work order rather than about the product. The plan reviewer refused version 3 because the `matches` branch was specified twice with contradictory readings: the prose said 'NO normalisation of the text', the code snippet said getNormalisedString(textValue), and the reason given ($regexp compiles to REGEXP(right, left) with neither /i nor NORMALISE) supports the prose. The reviewer was right, and I re-derived it from source rather than picking a side: compiler.ts:737-740 emits `REGEXP(right, left)` with no NORMALISE wrapper and no case flag, and the sqlite function it binds (sqlite/index.ts:218-234) is `new RegExp(regex).test(text || '')` over the un-normalised column - so the axis must be `new RegExp(value).test(textValue)` with nothing else. The snippet has been deleted and the branch now appears once, in both `approach` and `files[]`, with the same reading. The reviewer also pointed out that nothing in tests[] distinguished the two readings, which is correct - the listed case ('RE' returns []) passes under both, because lowercasing the text does not lowercase the pattern. A diacritic-bearing case has been added for exactly that purpose: with a fixture category named 'Café', value 'cafe' returns [] and value 'Café' returns ['cat-cafe'], which only the raw-text reading produces. I agree with that second point in full, and I did not try to solve it by widening AC-17 instead: AC-17 is deliberately one-directional because 'Show empty rows' legitimately renders categories the query returned nothing for, so a bidirectional criterion would be wrong. The test case is the right instrument. No acceptance criterion, file, test path or claim about BUG-2/BUG-3 is otherwise changed from version 3.",
  "root_cause": "The PR made the report's row axis narrow on the category conditions (narrowCategoriesByConditions in packages/desktop-client/src/components/reports/spreadsheets/budgetDataQuery.ts). Soundness of that narrowing rests on an unstated premise: that the axis's evaluation of a condition and the query's evaluation of the same condition select the same set. For the id-valued operators that holds - is/isNot compare category.id, oneOf/notOneOf test membership of it, and conditionsToAQL compiles them to $eq/$ne/$oneof over the same id column. For the three TEXT operators it does not, because the two sides implement them in different languages. The axis reads `contains` as textValue.toLowerCase().includes(condition.value.toLowerCase()) - a literal substring test. The query reads it as a pattern: transaction-rules.ts:641-649 turns `contains` on a type-'id' field into apply(field + '.name', '$like', '%' + value + '%'); compiler.ts:733-736 emits UNICODE_LIKE(getNormalisedString(right), NORMALISE(left)); and unicodeLike.ts:7-31 converts the pattern to a JavaScript regex in which '%' becomes '.*', '?' becomes '.', and a backslash escapes only '%', '?' and a literal backslash. Nothing escapes the user's value, so a value of '%' produces the pattern '%%%', which matches every category name. The axis simultaneously folds case with toLowerCase() on both sides while the query folds case AND diacritics (l/o/ss/oe plus NFD decomposition, shared/normalisation.ts), and the axis compiles `matches` with the /i flag while the query compiles $regexp with neither /i nor NORMALISE (compiler.ts:737-740). Three concrete consequences, all of them the axis being a strict SUBSET of the query, which is the direction that silently discards money: (1) `Category contains %` - BUG-2 - narrows the axis to zero rows under 'all of', with no 'any of' involved, while the query returns the whole budget; QA measured 0.00 on Split=Category against an unchanged baseline on Split=Payee in the same run, which is the differential that proves the query still holds the money and the axis threw it away. (2) the same condition under 'any of' with a second category disjunct - BUG-3 - returns true from canNarrowAxisByConditions because both disjuncts have field 'category', so the axis narrows to the Bills categories while the query's $or unions them and the '%' arm matches everything; 'Food' and every non-Bills category disappear and their spending is fetched and discarded. BUG-1's failure mode, re-entered through an operator the gate does not inspect. (3) `matches` with a value over 256 characters, which the axis rejects for every category via its own length guard while the query's REGEXP applies it - a third instance of the same shape that no browser case has yet reached. This is a defect INTRODUCED by this PR: before it the axis was unnarrowed under every condition set, which is a superset and therefore never loses money. It escaped the previous gate because that gate was written entirely along the field dimension and took the equality of the two readings as given. Verified by execution, not by reading the error string: the axis evaluation captured as served at the PR head (qa-evidence/qa-evidence/served-budgetDataQuery.ts:99-140) was run against the compiled query's evaluation over 21 category names and the full operator/value set. `contains` diverges on '%' (axis 1 of 21, query 21), '?' (0 of 21), '\\%' (0 of 1) and 'e' (missing 'Café'); `matches` diverges on 'RE', 're', 'C', 'café' and the 601-character value; while an evaluation built on the shared likePatternToRegex and getNormalisedString is byte-equal to the query on every value.",
  "approach": "Delete the divergence instead of screening for it. Two edits, and the second is the whole fix.\n\nFirst, move likePatternToRegex out of the SQLite platform layer and into shared code so there is exactly one implementation of the pattern language in the repository. Create packages/loot-core/src/shared/likePattern.ts exporting likePatternToRegex(pattern: string): RegExp, carrying over REGEX_SPECIAL and the translation loop verbatim from unicodeLike.ts:5-31, and have unicodeLike.ts import it instead of defining it. That file keeps its LRU cache, its null handling and its signature, so UNICODE_LIKE's behaviour is unchanged - its existing 12-case test file (unicodeLike.test.ts) is the guard that the move was faithful, and it must pass without being edited. Shared is the right home rather than an export from platform/server: desktop-client must not import another platform's modules, and @actual-app/core/shared/* is an existing export that desktop-client already imports from (ManageRules.tsx:14 imports getNormalisedString from there).\n\nSecond, and this is the fix, rewrite the three text-operator branches of evaluateCondition in budgetDataQuery.ts so the axis computes what the query computes, using the same two primitives and in the same order. `contains` becomes likePatternToRegex(getNormalisedString('%' + value + '%')).test(getNormalisedString(textValue)) - pattern folded and matched against text folded, which is literally what UNICODE_LIKE(getNormalisedString(pattern), NORMALISE(text)) does. `doesNotContain` is the negation of that same expression, because the query's $notlike compiles to NOT UNICODE_LIKE(...) OR left IS NULL and category.name is never NULL.\n\n`matches` becomes `new RegExp(condition.value).test(textValue)` and nothing else - no getNormalisedString on either side, no /i flag, no length guard. That is a single reading, stated once because the query admits only one: $regexp compiles to `REGEXP(right, left)` (compiler.ts:737-740) with neither NORMALISE nor a case flag, and the sqlite `regexp` it reaches is `new RegExp(regex).test(text || '')` (sqlite/index.ts:218-234) - raw pattern against raw text, case-sensitive, diacritics intact, at any pattern length. So the axis passes `textValue` (the untouched `category.name`, or the group name) through unchanged. Note what this does NOT say: there is no getNormalisedString in the `matches` branch, and there is no /i. An earlier draft of this order asserted that in prose and then wrote a snippet that called getNormalisedString(textValue); the two readings produce different code and only one of them is correct, so the snippet is gone and this sentence replaces it.\n\nNote what this does NOT need: no new operator conditions in canNarrowAxisByConditions, no change to the gate, no change to the query side, no change to any call site. The gate was never the wrong thing - it asks whether the disjuncts are about the axis, which is still the right question - it was relying on an equality that did not hold. Once the equality holds by construction, the `contains` disjunct in BUG-3 makes the axis return every category and the query return every transaction, and both agree. AC-13's negative control still passes for the same reason it passed on this head: 'or' over two genuinely axis-bound conditions still narrows to their union.\n\nEvery other file, and every criterion AC-1 through AC-13, stays exactly as QA last saw them.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "packages/loot-core/src/shared/likePattern.ts",
      "action": "create",
      "change": "New module exporting `export function likePatternToRegex(pattern: string): RegExp`. Move the translation loop and its REGEX_SPECIAL regex verbatim from packages/loot-core/src/platform/server/sqlite/unicodeLike.ts:5-31, unchanged and in the same order: a backslash escapes only '%', '?' and a literal backslash (consuming two characters); '%' becomes '.*'; '?' becomes '.'; every other character is emitted with REGEX_SPECIAL applied; the result is `new RegExp(regexStr, 'i')`. Do NOT add underscore handling, wildcard escaping of the user's value, or any other behaviour - the semantics of this function are the query's semantics, and unicodeLike's own tests pin them. Header comment stating that this is the single definition of the LIKE pattern language and that anything which must agree with UNICODE_LIKE must go through it.",
      "why": "The pattern language currently exists once, inside the SQLite platform layer, which is exactly why the report's row axis could not be made to agree with the query: the desktop app has no lawful way to reach it. Putting it in shared/ is what makes 'agree by construction' possible rather than 'agree by careful duplication'."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/loot-core/src/platform/server/sqlite/unicodeLike.ts",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Delete the local REGEX_SPECIAL and likePatternToRegex definitions (lines 5-31) and add `import { likePatternToRegex } from '#shared/likePattern';`. Keep the lru-cache instance, the exported unicodeLike signature, the `if (!pattern) return 0` and `if (!value) value = ''` guards and the cache lookup exactly as they are - only the pattern construction is delegated. The file's existing test (unicodeLike.test.ts, 12 cases covering wildcards, '?', escaped wildcards, doubled backslash, unicode case folding and null handling) must pass with no edit to it; that suite is the proof this change is behaviour-preserving.",
      "why": "One implementation, so the axis cannot drift from the query later. This is a move, not a change: everything that reads UNICODE_LIKE - index.ts, index.electron.ts and index.api.ts (which re-exports the electron one) - keeps identical behaviour."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/desktop-client/src/components/reports/spreadsheets/budgetDataQuery.ts",
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "In evaluateCondition, replace the three text-operator branches so each computes what the query computes, and add imports of getNormalisedString from '@actual-app/core/shared/normalisation' and likePatternToRegex from '@actual-app/core/shared/likePattern'. `contains`: return likePatternToRegex(getNormalisedString('%' + condition.value + '%')).test(getNormalisedString(textValue)). `doesNotContain`: return the negation of that same expression - the query's $notlike compiles to NOT UNICODE_LIKE(...) OR left IS NULL and category.name is never NULL, so the negation is exact. `matches`: return new RegExp(condition.value).test(textValue) - the raw pattern against the raw textValue, with NO getNormalisedString call anywhere in the branch, NO /i flag, and the `condition.value.length <= 256` clause deleted outright. This mirrors $regexp, which compiles to `REGEXP(right, left)` (compiler.ts:737-740) with neither NORMALISE nor a case flag and reaches the sqlite `regexp` (sqlite/index.ts:218-234) that is `new RegExp(regex).test(text || '')` on the un-normalised column. Read that as the whole rule: raw pattern, raw text, no length limit. Do not write getNormalisedString(textValue) here and do not keep the /i flag; an earlier draft of this order specified the branch twice with those two readings and the reviewer refused it as unfollowable. Keep the try/catch around the regex construction so an invalid pattern still evaluates false, which is what the query's REGEXP does (sqlite/index.ts:218-234). Leave is/isNot/oneOf/notOneOf byte-identical - they compare the same category.id the query compares - and leave isSupportedCategoryCondition, canNarrowAxisByConditions, narrowCategoriesByConditions, filterCategoriesByConditions's conservative fallback and every call site untouched. Comment each rewritten branch with the query-side line it mirrors so the next reader knows the pairing is deliberate and can check it when either side changes."
    }
  ],
  "tests": [
    {
      "path": "packages/loot-core/src/shared/likePattern.test.ts",
