        "`matches` is case-SENSITIVE like the query: value 'RE' returns [] for the fixture, while the PR head's /i returns ['cat-rent']. An invalid pattern such as '(' still evaluates to false rather than throwing. THE READING-AMBIGUITY CASE, and the reason it exists: a fixture category literally named 'Café' - value 'cafe' returns [] against it (the query does no diacritic folding on $regexp, so 'cafe' does not match 'Café') while value 'Café' returns ['cat-cafe']. This is the single case that separates the two candidate readings of the `matches` branch: getNormalisedString(textValue) would match 'cafe' against 'Café' and the query would not, so an implementation that normalises the text fails here. It is here because the money-oriented cases above cannot tell the readings apart - the existing 'RE' case passes under both, since lowercasing the text does not lowercase the pattern.",
